<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kowloon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kowloon is an urban area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> on the peninsula facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> across Victoria Harbour. Its southern tip was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1860 under the Convention of Peking, and the lands to the north were leased in 1898 as part of the New Territories. It is one of the most densely populated places in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kowloon.jpg" alt="Kowloon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wishva de Silva · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Union_Square_and_Tsim_Sha_Tsui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kowloon, formerly spelled Kaulung, is one of the three areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> and the New Territories. It lies directly north of Hong Kong Island across Victoria Harbour, bordered by the Lei Yue Mun strait to the east and by a mountain range including Tate's Cairn and Lion Rock to the north. The Chinese name means Nine Dragons and alludes to eight mountains and a Chinese emperor: Kowloon Peak, Tung Shan, Tate's Cairn, Temple Hill, Unicorn Ridge, Lion Rock, Beacon Hill, Crow's Nest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Emperor Bing of Song</a>. It is an urban area made up of the Kowloon Peninsula and New Kowloon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The part of Kowloon south of Boundary Street, together with Stonecutters Island, was ceded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> to the United Kingdom under the Convention of Peking of 1860. For many years it stayed largely undeveloped, and the British used it mainly for tiger-hunting expeditions. The part north of Boundary Street, called New Kowloon, was leased to the British for 99 years as part of the New Territories under the Second Convention of Peking of 1898. Within New Kowloon lay the Kowloon Walled City, which was demolished in 1993. The same area was called Kwun Fu Cheung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>.</p><p>Large-scale development began in the early 20th century with the Kowloon-Canton Railway and the Kowloon Wharf. Flight paths into Kai Tak Airport limited building heights, so Kowloon kept a much lower skyline than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a>. West Kowloon was once the site of a dockyard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1911 recorded 7,306 people, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1937, about 750,000 refugees arrived in Kowloon and nearby areas between 1937 and 1939, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> slums housing refugees from the newly established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> gave way to public housing estates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>By statute Kowloon is only the area south of Boundary Street and Stonecutters Island, but in common use New Kowloon is counted as part of the Kowloon urban area. Its five districts are Kowloon City, Kwun Tong, Sham Shui Po, Wong Tai Sin and Yau Tsim Mong. Cantonese is the usual language of about 86 per cent of residents. Road and rail tunnels connect Kowloon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a>, and no bridges do.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kowloon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kowloon</b> was added in 1860, and in 1898 the New Territories were leased for ninety-nine years, giving the colony its final shape within the British Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/hong-kong-british">Hong Kong (British)</a></li></ul></section>
